    "lawCode": "Amd §§693, 694 & 695, Gen Muni L",
    "lawSection": "General Municipal Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5891\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the general municipal law, in relation to urban\ndevelopment action areas\n\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nExisting provisions of Article 16 of the General Municipal law\nauthorize expedited land use review under Urban Development Action\nArea Project (UDAAP) for projects to rehabilitate existing residential\nbuildings or to construct one- to four-unit dwellings. This bill would\nexpand this expedited land use review provision to include federally\nfinanced projects to construct new rental housing for elderly or\ndisabled persons of low income. Chapter 701 of the Laws of 2006 was\nintended to create such a result but several sections of the General\nMunicipal Law that are required to effectuate this purpose were\ninadvertently omitted from that Chapter. This bill contains those\nprovisions and would now allow the Section 202 and Section 811\nprojects to be subject to accelerated land use review as was intended\nby Chapter 701.\n\n REASONS FOR SUPPORT :\n\nThis bill would strongly support the development of federally financed\nhousing for elderly and disabled persons of low income by making such\nprojects eligible for an expedited land use review under UDAAP. Under\nSection 202, the Supportive Housing Program for the Elderly, and\nSection 811, the Supportive Housing Program for Persons with\nDisabilities, not-for-profit sponsors purchase City-owned land and\nconstruct new multiple dwellings with financing from the United States\nDepartment of Housing and Urban Development (HUD). The completed\nbuildings provide rental housing for elderly or disabled persons of\nlow income and receive operating subsidies from HUD. Since 2002, HUD\nhas awarded Section 202/811 funding for over 1,900 units in the five\nboroughs most of which have been constructed on city-owned property.\nOver 95% of these units were allocated to the low income elderly and\nthe majority are located in areas of Brooklyn and the Bronx where the\ndemand was greatest for this desperately needed, but scarce, housing\nresource. Total HUD funding for these projects exceeded $230 million.\n\nCurrently, the sale of City-owned land for these projects is subject\nto the Uniform Land Use Review Procedure, (ULURP), which delays the\ndevelopment of affordable housing for these vulnerable populations.  A\nland use action subject to the full ULURP process can take up to 11\nmonths to complete. Delays of this length often have serious\nconsequences for these federally funded projects. HUD funding for\ndevelopments and construction cost are low for typical projects in New\nYork City market. Once allocated, however, the HUD funding timeframes\nare not negotiable. Adding time to the development process causes\ncosts to rise and leaves the City in the position (having to fill the\ngap with other funding sources. This, in turn, makes the development\nprocess even longer. By allowing Section 202 and Section 811 projects\nto be reviewed under UDAAP, the land use approval process will take a\nmaximum of 150 days while still retaining the City Council's full land\nuse review authority. As a result, UDAAP land use review, instead of\nULURP, would reduce the approval process by six to nine months and\nhelp ensure the timely and successful completion of the sorely needed\nhousing developments.\n\nThe ability to accelerate the land use approval process of these\ndevelopments currently sunsets on June 30, 2009 and no more than 6\nsuch developments annually will be subject to this expedited land use\nprocess. Development subject to this authority cannot exceed over 90\ndwelling units in size. This bill extends the authority to accelerate\nthis review until June 30, 2012 and adds the authorizing language to\nseveral sections of the General Municipal law that were accidentally\nomitted in 2006.\n\n\nIn summary, the bill would not only speed up the development of\nhousing for low-income seniors and the disabled, but would also reduce\nconstruction costs and ensure that scarce and highly competitive\nfederal funding commitments are not jeopardized by unwarranted delays.\n\nAccordingly, the Mayor urges the earliest possible favorable\nconsideration of this proposal by the Legislature.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
